---
title: Space for TV
authors: [Elias Herder]
broadcast: 6
order: 3
---

Talk shows originate from in the 1930s and 1940s, where the format centered on interviews, discussions, and audience participation. The genre migrated to television in the 1950s, with programs like The Tonight Show helping to establish the late-night format we recognize today. Daytime talk shows rose to prominence between the 1960s and 1980s, focusing on celebrity interviews, personal stories, and social issues. In the 1990s, the genre shifted towards more controversial topics and greater audience involvement. Among the most successful talk shows in history is The Late Show, which has been broadcast from New York City’s Ed Sullivan Theater.

The Ed Sullivan Theater carries its own storied history. Built in 1927 as a Broadway venue, it later became home to The Jackie Gleason Show and The Ed Sullivan Show—the latter famous for hosting the Beatles’ first American television performance in 1964. The Late show has occupied the theater for the last thirty years.

Reflecting the operatic background of founder Oscar Hammerstein, the theater is a dramatic, neo-Gothic space. The Late Show’s set design subtly echoes its surrounding architecture while creating a more intimate environment for conversation. The theater’s stained glass windows are reimagined as customizable stage lighting, while its columns are recreated in exact proportion to frame the set. To challenge the scale of the theater, the stage is subdivided into alcoves.

