Sitcoms—short for situational comedies—originated on radio in the 1920s and 1930s before gaining widespread popularity on television in the 1950s. Early shows like I Love Lucy helped establish the format’s defining elements: recurring characters, everyday situations, and comedic storytelling.

From the 1960s to the 1980s, sitcoms increasingly explored family life, workplace dynamics, and social issues, all while retaining their comedic core. Friends, broadcast from 1994 to 2004, was filmed at Warner Bros. Studios in Burbank, California, though it was set in New York City—following a group of friends as they navigate the ups and downs of life in the Big Apple.

Filmed inside an apartment set built in a studio nearly 2,000 miles from its supposed location, the show is, in many ways, built on illusion. The apartment set lacks a fourth wall, allowing a live studio audience to watch the show unfold in real time. Lighting create the illusion of an exterior environment, recreating conditions like rain or sunlight as needed. Even the set itself isn’t quite as it appears—walls shift or disappear entirely.

