Community access television is typically created by and for local civilians and emerged from disenchantment with establishment broadcasting. Community access television spread across the nation when the FCC issued the Third Report and Order in 1972, mandating the top hundred cable tv companies to include public access television networks. Community programs can consist of anything from talent shows to reporting on social justice.

Community access tv has its downsides. Not everyone ends up with equal access to broadcasting time, and sometimes executives can get in the way. Worse yet, fringe and explicit content has also found a place in community television. Overall, programs range from the fringe, to the bizarre, to the surreal, to the educational, to the benevolent.

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/elias-tv-07.jpg" alt="A man in a suit and green rubber gloves holding up a small painting, with a call-in phone number along the bottom of the screen" pie="1.1 Lets Paint TV" /%}
