{% /galeria %}

Nevertheless, community access programming is a grassroots alternative media which has since become a global phenomenon. As we move into the digital age, community access television has adapted to online programming.

{% figura src="/images/vignettes/elias-tv-10.jpg" alt="Screenshot of BRIC's website listing its online community television programs as a grid of thumbnails" pie="BRIC Online Community TV offerings" /%}

There are many spaces for community access television across the country, one of the first of which is Manhattan Neighborhood Network in New York City. Spaces for community access programs are decentralized, consisting of various rooms for editing, producing and filming. The sets for filming are by their nature adaptable to support different, often disparate shows.

{% galeria %}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/elias-tv-11.jpg" alt="A small podcast studio with microphones on boom arms, cameras and a green curtain" pie="3.2 Podcast studio available for rent" /%}
{% figura src="/images/vignettes/elias-tv-12.jpg" alt="An editing room with a desk of monitors and chairs against yellow and gray walls" pie="3.2 Editing Studios Available for Rent." /%}
{% /galeria %}

In the contemporary era, the distinctions between different archetypes of television content have grown increasingly blurred. As the internet has become woven into everyday life, TV programming has adapted, with many programs migrating online. Manhattan Neighborhood Network has digitized its programs, The Late Show maintains an active YouTube channel, and countless streaming series now exist that never held a place on traditional cable television at all. This growing homogeneity invites a broader question: what role do physical spaces—like the Ed Sullivan Theater, or the sets of Friends—still play for television in a digital age?
